\section*{Discussion}

\subsection*{Why the Shorter Window Performed Better}

The results support a specific conclusion: adding earlier catalog decades
changed the fitted branching process enough to cross the stationarity boundary.
The 1960 fits used more than three times as many target events as the 2000 fit,
yet all were supercritical. Raising the threshold did not correct the problem,
and broad perturbations to the starting values converged to the same solution.
The likely cause is nonstationarity in either seismicity, observation, or both,
rather than numerical instability alone.

Several mechanisms are plausible. Network coverage and magnitude practices have
changed since 1960. A fixed national $M_c$ cannot fully represent temporal and
spatial detection heterogeneity, and ETAS parameters are known to be sensitive
to truncation and missing events \citep{Seif2017,Mizrahi2021b}. A single
homogeneous background intensity must also explain a region spanning distinct
tectonic environments. The empirical background-location sampler captures
where inferred background events occurred, but it does not introduce
time-varying tectonic rates. Finally, a single stationary parameter vector must
represent decades containing major sequences and quieter intervals. The
shorter window may better match the post-2021 observation process and recent
rate regime, even though it has higher parameter uncertainty.

This interpretation does not imply that 2000 is a universally optimal start
date. It means only that, among the tested configurations at one forecast
origin, it was the sole admissible fit and the least biased over five years.
A proper training-window study should repeat the analysis over many forecast
origins and separate catalog-era effects from individual earthquake sequences.

\subsection*{Consistency Is Not the Same as Skill}

The 2000-window model passed 17 of 20 configured horizon-test combinations, but
that number overstates the independent evidence. The horizons share most of
their observations, and every model was selected and ranked after inspecting
the same 2021--2025 catalog. Furthermore, no reference forecast was scored.
Consistency asks whether an observation is plausible under a model; skill asks
whether one forecast predicts better than another. These are not equivalent
\citep{Zechar2013,Savran2022}.

The first-year Kermadec underforecast and subsequent overprediction illustrate
why horizon-resolved diagnostics matter. At one year the model missed a major
offshore sequence and underpredicted the national count by 145.5 events. By
three years, cumulative counts nearly matched. By five years, the model
overpredicted by 168.0 events. A single average count ratio or composite score
compresses this sign change and can obscure the process error.

\subsection*{Implications for Operational Forecasting}

Three safeguards follow directly from the run. First, branching-ratio
admissibility should be a hard precondition, not a soft diagnostic. The
override was useful for research because it revealed the divergence pattern,
but supercritical candidates should not enter an operational ensemble without
an explicit finite-horizon or constrained formulation. Second, maximum
magnitude must be specified or inferred from a defensible tapered distribution.
An $M11.2$ realization is not a useful expression of epistemic uncertainty for
this domain. Third, spatial background structure should be tested against an
independent long-term model rather than inferred only from the same catalog used
to fit triggering.

Parameter uncertainty also remains absent. All simulations condition on a
single maximum-likelihood estimate. Bayesian or likelihood-profile approaches
could propagate this uncertainty, although at greater computational cost. In
this experiment the difference between $n=0.969$ and unity is small enough that
uncertainty in $n$ is operationally important.

\subsection*{Limitations}

This analysis has six principal limitations. (1) It uses one retrospective
forecast origin; it cannot establish prospective performance. (2) Horizons are
nested, so cross-horizon pass counts and calibration statistics are dependent.
(3) The sweep explored only seven hand-selected configurations and used a
post-hoc composite score. (4) Fixed completeness thresholds were assumed rather
than estimated with uncertainty. (5) No maximum magnitude, fault geometry,
depth dependence, or parameter uncertainty was included. (6) random seeds were
not fixed, although 2,000 archived realizations reduce Monte Carlo noise for
the reported ensemble summaries.

There is also a reproducibility distinction between data and experiment state.
The GeoNet catalog is continually revised, and a fresh query may not reproduce
the exact input file. The archived \texttt{nzcat.csv}, parameter files,
simulation catalogs, and software revision are therefore part of the
computational record. Future experiments should version the catalog query,
record event-product revisions, and use deterministic seed sequences.

\section*{Conclusions}

A New Zealand-wide ETAS calibration sweep showed that training-window choice
controlled both the mathematical admissibility of the fitted process and its
retrospective calibration. Six configurations trained from 1960 or 1980 were
supercritical despite changes in magnitude threshold and initial values. The
2000--2020 model was the only subcritical fit and produced the closest
cumulative count trajectory over 2021--2025. Even this model strongly
underpredicted the first-year Kermadec sequence, shifted to longer-term
overprediction, and generated nonphysical upper-magnitude realizations because
the Gutenberg--Richter distribution was unbounded.

The appropriate conclusion is not that the 2000-window model is validated for
operations. Rather, the experiment demonstrates a reproducible screening
workflow: reject or constrain supercritical inversions, inspect calibration as
a function of horizon and location, impose a defensible magnitude tail, and
repeat the comparison at independent forecast origins. Those steps are
necessary before the apparent consistency of a catalog ensemble can be
interpreted as forecast skill.

